  {
   "id": "5ArRjjuyCQAJ",
   "from": "Daniel Morillo Cuadrado",
   "author": "38226bd700051927",
   "date": "2024-05-27T10:12:04Z",
   "subject": "Re: Thresholds do not correspond to Item difficulty (Pi)",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Classical Test Theory item difficulties are the proportion of correct responses in your sample (not the SD of that proportion, watch out for that). They are a &quot;raw measure&quot; of difficulty, if you want to see it like that.</div><div><br></div><div>Item thresholds in CFA correspond to difficulty parameters in Item Response Theory, under the following conditions:</div><div><br></div><div>- Latent variables are (equally) standardized. In your case, as you have (by default) fixed the factor loading of A_1 to 1, the latent variable of &quot;kon&quot; is not standardized. You can change this default by calling `cfa()` with argument `<a href=\"http://std.lv\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://std.lv&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792414520539000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1kGWmC0A6R45wKx2lbZoy8\">std.lv</a> = TRUE`.</div><div>- The parameterization of both models is equal. You can change the default (&quot;theta&quot; parameterization) by calling \r\n`cfa()` with argument `parameterization = &quot;delta&quot;`. This way it gives you the values in the &quot;logistic&quot; metric, as you would expect in an IRT model.</div><div><br></div><div>Then you can try fitting (e.g. with package {mirt}) a 2-parameter logistic IRT model (with standardized latent variance), and check whether thresholds and item difficulties match.</div><div><br></div><div>What you can try with what you have is correlating the thresholds with the CTT item difficulties; you will probably find a relatively high (negative*) correlation.<br>\r\n\r\n</div><div><div><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><font size=\"2\"><span><br></span></font></div><div><font size=\"2\"><span>Hope that helps!</span></font></div><div><font size=\"2\"><span><br></span></font></div><div><font size=\"2\"><span>*It should be negative because CTT &quot;item difficulty&quot;, as it the proportion of correct responses, is higher the easier the item is. So in fact it should be understood as a measure of &quot;item easiness&quot;.<br></span></font></div><div><font size=\"2\"><span><br></span></font></div><div><font size=\"2\"><span>--</span></font></div><div><font size=\"2\"><span style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif\">Daniel Morillo, Ph.D.</span></font></div><div><font size=\"2\"><span style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif\"></span></font></div><font size=\"2\"><span style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif\">\r\n<a href=\"https://github.com/DaniMori\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/DaniMori&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792414520539000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1O_aBKg8ZYepvSPXSPl43-\">GitHub</a> | <a href=\"https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3021-3878\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3021-3878&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792414520539000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0JZh87G4mvcDkWBjdFgfkH\">\r\nORCID</a></span></font></div></div></div></div></div></div><br></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
